WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.899
Hello everybody and welcome to today's NCBI
webinar.

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:08.090
We will talk about the Genome Data Viewer
today.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.030
This slide has preliminary information you
may need.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:17.980
Wayne Matten is here with me and he will answer
questions during the webinar when they come

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:18.980
up.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.080
All the questions and answers will be available
after the webinar in a document that will

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.720
be linked to our webinars and courses page.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.500
It will also be available in our FTP directory
for this particular webinar, that's the materials

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.190
directory there is a compressed URL that will
take you to the directory.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.190
Let's get started.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.870
I will show you up few slides and then we
will do a live demo for the rest of the time.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.260
The Genome Data Viewer is the topic today
and mostly today I will say GDV when I mean

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.430
the Genome Data Viewer because it's faster
. My name is Peter Cooper and if you have

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.460
questions you can write to me at the address
at the bottom of the slide.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.260
peter.cooper@nih.gov
We will talk about what the Genome Data Viewer

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.199
is and we will talk about accessing it.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.590
We will do a brief overview of the interface
and functions and mostly we will demonstrate

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.270
it live for you today.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:19.240
The Genome Data Viewer is now NCBI's main
genome browser and the assembly browser for

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.899
eukaryotic organizations and this replaces
the map viewer which was the original genome

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:24.939
browser.

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:29.069
You will recognize the interface which is
similar to other specialized browsers we've

00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:34.460
had available like the 1000 genomes browser
and the variation viewer.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:40.091
Embedded in the center of all of those browsers
and familiar I hope to you is the sequence

00:01:40.091 --> 00:01:45.399
viewer panel which is present in other places
like Gene and Nucleotide graphics view.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:51.320
It has functions for navigating across the
assembly, uploading mapped data and accessing

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.119
analysis tools.

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:57.840
Right now in the Genome Data Viewer we have
over 500 eukaryotic RefSeq genomes available.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.009
How do you access it?

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:05.029
There is a homepage that you can easily get
to and the quickest way to find it is search

00:02:05.029 --> 00:02:13.540
in Google and it has by default you search
human genome and you can use this tree function

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:21.950
to browse various organisms that you can load
into the Genome Data Viewer.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:29.360
For example I can expand the node there to
see zebrafish and other bony fishes and it's

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.250
a convenient way to navigate and browse by
organism.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:39.510
Today we will focus on a particular assembly,
actually more than one for a particular organism

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.410
and that is the human genome.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:47.260
You can search with the gene names in the
box, SNP IDs, RefSeq accessions, or chromosome

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.260
positions.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:54.491
You could also pick assembly listed here,
the current reference assembly, GRCh38. or

00:02:54.491 --> 00:02:57.880
the previous assembly, GRCh37.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:03.950
You can also search alternate assemblies,
the HuReF assembly and also the hydatid molar

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.540
cell line assembly.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:12.300
There is a browser function where you can
enter the browser itself with chromosome 1

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.110
and you can BLAST a specific database that
contains a particular assembly you are interested

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:23.850
in and this ideogram view allows you to click
and browse a particular chromosome that you

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:24.880
would like.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:31.210
Another way to get to GDV and these have been
around for a while in various places at NCBI.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:36.460
So from the Gene I can link to the Genome
Data Viewer and in Assembly I can link to

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.450
and browse in the Genome Data Viewer.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:46.540
And today as a matter of fact we now have
links from BLAST to the Genome Data Viewer

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.720
which went live this morning.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.380
Here is the main browser interface and it
should look familiar to you.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.370
Sort of in the middle of it is the graphical
sequence viewer and on the left-hand side

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:04.400
at the top are various search and navigation
features.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.640
If you run a search you can get results in
the ideogram view and it shows you hits on

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.690
various chromosomes.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:15.900
You might find hits on more than one place
as it is a text search.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.269
You can select the one you want from the panel
underneath it.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.669
There are widgets for uploading the data,
adding tracks, loading BLAST results and we

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:28.069
will use those in a few moments when we do
the demonstration.

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:33.259
At the top there are navigation functions
and there is a gene and Exon navigator which

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:35.740
you can take advantage of.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:41.720
At the very top there is an ideogram with
the chromosome you are on and viewing at this

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.659
moment is chromosome 1 and you can use that
as a way to navigate and display various parts

00:04:46.659 --> 00:04:49.340
of the chromosome.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.620
Just a little detail on additional functions
and we will use these today and hopefully

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.050
we get a chance to use them all but might
not get to all of them.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:03.280
I can upload my own data and these are the
various kinds of files types listed here on

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.340
the slide, BED, GFF, GTF, VCF, HGVS.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:13.639
I can also use an URL or simply paste the
data in the text box.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:18.080
I can run BLAST from within the browser and
view the alignments there, and there is a

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.080
little tool, the alignment inspector lets
me navigate across my BLAST matches using

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.080
either the query coordinate system or the
geneome coordinate system.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.819
I can look at assembly region details.

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:35.659
There are places where we have alternate assemblies
for the human genome because we can't represent

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:42.770
the diversity of the human genome in one particular
assembly so we have ways of browsing those.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:48.029
Finally there are some NCBI data sets that
are mapped to the assembly and you can load

00:05:48.029 --> 00:05:50.529
those to the add tracks function.

00:05:50.529 --> 00:05:55.310
You can add all the tracks that are available
in the sequence viewer.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:56.939
Let's do some live examples.

00:05:56.939 --> 00:06:03.219
I will focus on two chromosomes and a couple
of different things on one and an area on

00:06:03.219 --> 00:06:04.219
another.

00:06:04.219 --> 00:06:09.979
First, we will look at the areas surrounding
the Adenosine Deaminase gene on chromosome

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:16.009
20 a we will add recommended tracksets that
NCBI has and configure the tracks that are

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:17.009
there.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:21.699
We will upload custom data and show you how
to print and share views from that and from

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:28.210
that particular gene we will run Primer-BLAST
to get primers to amplify an Exon, then we

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:35.280
will BLAST some sequences from different species
and load those into the browser to look at

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.169
conservation.

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:44.020
And on chromosome 2 if we get a chance we
will take a look at region 116 which has alternate

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:49.780
assemblies and we will look at the region
surrounding the Lactase gene . In the Lactase

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:56.860
gene we will look at gene expression tracks
as well as ones I made myself from the tracks

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.789
available in the sequence viewer that have
to do with Exon coverage.

00:07:01.789 --> 00:07:08.839
And then we change assemblies and move from
38 back to 37 and we can upload an SRA data

00:07:08.839 --> 00:07:11.580
set.

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:21.740
By the way these are all based loosely on
a set of demos that Wayne did at ASHG a couple

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:22.939
of weeks ago.

00:07:22.939 --> 00:07:26.949
If you want to get that particular handout,
which is a nice PDF, there is a link at the

00:07:26.949 --> 00:07:28.840
bottom of the slide that will take you there.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:29.840
https://go.usa.gov/xn2R4

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.589
I will escape out of the web browser.

00:07:33.589 --> 00:07:37.619
Here is the Genome Data Viewer interface.

00:07:37.619 --> 00:07:40.860
You can pick a particular organism and we
selected human for you.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.719
If I want to select a different one I can
do that here.

00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:50.749
I can click to expand the nodes as I showed
on the slide a few minutes ago to look for

00:07:50.749 --> 00:07:53.629
a particular organism in here.

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:57.759
If I want to search the human genome here
is the way to do it.

00:07:57.759 --> 00:08:02.270
I can browse the genome or BLAST against this
assembly and we can pick the assembly here

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.610
as I dscused.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.710
I can search for lots of different markers
on the genome.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:19.050
I will search for Adenosine Deanminase so
let's run that search.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:26.069
Here we have the region surrounding Adenosine
Deanminase with the default tracks available

00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:27.069
here.

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:34.150
Adenosine Deanminase is a gene associated
with severe human phenotypes if there are

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:39.120
mutations, one is severe combined immunodeficiency.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.390
Let's say we are interested in some kind of
clinical features of this particular gene.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:49.840
I can load the clinical features track by
going up here to the tracks menu and I can

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.850
go to the NCBI recommended tracksets here.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:58.520
I will load the clinical tracks.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:05.330
We now have the gene map on top for Adenosine
Deanminase and I loaded a number of other

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:06.330
tracks.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:12.190
This is an interesting one we will focus on
which is the RefSeqGene alignment to the assembly

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:18.280
and it's interesting because these are particular
reagents or records designed specifically

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:24.620
to give you a stable coordinate system with
canonical sequences aligned to them and even

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:27.900
has the Exons spelled out on this transcript.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:35.470
So we will use that as a way of mapping out
where we are on the Exons of this gene.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.580
Down here are all the variants from dbSNP.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:44.000
Under that are variations from the ClinVar,
our clinical database that talks about the

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.060
relationships between variations and human
phenotypes.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:52.370
ANd then there are other types of variation
tracks that are large-scale variants, insertions

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.780
and duplications and things like that.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:59.680
There are other tracks that have nothing in
this particular area so I will delete these

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:00.680
tracks.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:06.990
That's one thing we can do to modify the display
settings.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:13.740
Now I have a slightly simplified display.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:18.460
Notice the track for the RefSeqGene record
has an expanded view.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:23.560
I have a gene bar and the transcript and protein
sequence shown here.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.580
Transcript in blue, the protein is in red.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:31.770
It might be nice to have this gene view up
here so the Adenosine Deanminase is rendered

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.280
in the same way.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.430
You can do that by going to the tracks menu
and configuring the tracks or also configure

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:44.640
the tracks by mousing over the top of the
track and using the gear icon or you can use

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.810
a shortcut which is this button that looks
like the tracks and if you click that you

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:55.960
will get various transcripts and protein sequences
shown.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:00.920
One thing to notice is the transcripts are
shown with a gray background and if you go

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:07.740
to the help document for this track you realize
that's because there are mismatches compared

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:08.740
to the genome.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:14.060
If I mouse over one of these it will tell
me that.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:17.780
This transcript has one substitution compared
to this genomic sequence.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:22.630
That's because the transcript sequences here
are based on mRNA sequences in GenBank, the

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:26.990
genome may have a different variant in it.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:37.720
Notice there is a red line on the gene record
that shows that it has the mismatch, so it

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.480
matches that mRNA sequence.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.360
We can do something interesting and zoom in.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:50.830
We will zoom in to Exon 2 and I will zoom
to sequence.

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:57.510
So we are zoomed in to the region showing
that particular change between the RefSeqGene

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:00.300
record and the genome.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:05.310
This mismatch is at the position of a C/T
variant and you see the variant is listed

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:06.310
here.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:11.480
It is a common variant and you can add a track
to show that.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:15.990
One thing I noticed about this gene that makes
it a little bit confusing to look at is, notice

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:20.910
this basically is written backwards because
it's on the negative strand of the genome.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:24.110
I could flip it around if I want to.

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.660
Let's go to the tracks menu and add another
track.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:29.740
We go to configure tracks.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:35.320
Now I have the opportunity to choose from
a range of tracks.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.120
Let's go to variation tracks.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:43.230
Some of these are already shown and are checked.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:48.160
One that is not shown on the clinical track
are the common variants and you see the ones

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:56.440
with global minor allele frequencies of 1%
or more so those would be what we would call

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:02.840
polymorphisms.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:08.370
You can see indeed this variation that is
present in this person's genome that was used

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:14.140
to assemble this part of the genome has a
variant there.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:19.120
It is different from and if you look at the
minor allele frequency this actually the minor

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:20.701
allele at this position.

00:13:20.701 --> 00:13:27.010
It is a synonymous substitution that doesn't
change the valine code to a different amino

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:28.980
acid.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.160
There are other variants here and these color
codes here and you can look at that by getting

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:35.850
the help document for this particular track.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:39.620
Purple means they are pathogenic variants
and if you go to ClinVar you realize this

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:44.680
particular one is associated with severe combined
immunodeficiency.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:50.420
Let's go ahead and now we will do something
a little bit different and let's add some

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.420
data ourselves.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:57.590
We will go to the Your Data widget on the
left-hand side.

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:08.780
I am going to click this button to add files.

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.100
This is my file.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:20.170
This is from the HapMap individual NA12878
and it is a list of HGVS renderings of SNPs

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:25.490
in this particular region for this Gene and
I got this from the genome in a bottle data

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:31.510
available on the NCBI website.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:36.000
Those variants are showing up for me now in
this track at the bottom and I could have

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.801
named the track something more informative
than the name of the file.

00:14:39.801 --> 00:14:47.220
You see that person which is from the Utah
population has that SNP as well.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:53.320
You can navigate along these if you want to
to see where they go, where they are.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:15:00.140
Notice some of them are present in dbSNP and
this one in this particular position of the

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:06.440
genome is not present in SNP, so if it's not
an error, then it's a SNP that's not present

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:10.810
in the database.

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:20.920
I can use my Exon navigator to go back to
the Exon 2 we were looking at . Suppose I

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:26.190
like this and interested in saving it and
showing it to someone else, how can I do that?

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:27.680
There are two things you can do.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:36.590
One is go to the tools menu and click on the
link that says print friendly PDF and you

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:42.090
can create a PDF file right here.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.090
And I can view that.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:50.060
That is a nice graphic I can use to make a
figure if I want.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:59.290
The other option you have is to go up here
and click on the link that says share this

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:06.060
page and that gives me a URL I can share with
people and that's good for about 90 days.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:10.370
Let's go on to use some analysis tools within
the browser and let's move to a different

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:11.370
Exon.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:13.110
I will close that.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:18.780
I will go over here to Exon four and I will
center this a little better.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:26.460
and it is a Exon that has some disease gene
SNPs in it and you can see the pathogenic

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:27.930
SNPs listed here.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:34.370
If I want to amplify this, to get primers
that will amplify this exon, I can easily

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:35.370
do that.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:44.300
I go to tools and go to BLAST and primer search
and I will primer BLAST the visible range.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:51.920
It opens up Primer-BLAST and if I want to
a reasonable thing to do is adjust these ranges

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:57.790
so my primers are binding outside the Exon.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:01.120
And I can pick the database, in this case
I want to amplify this from genomic DNA and

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:04.839
it is automatically set for me to Homo sapiens.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:10.040
If I do that, and just to save time I will
show you what the results look like.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.819
so we have a set of primers here.

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:13.380
I can load those.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:16.990
After I run this they will be available to
me in the graphical sequencer viewer.

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:25.100
If I go to the tracks menu and go to configure
tracks, you notice there is a section called

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:28.610
Primer-BLAST.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:37.680
I can load those and I have several of them
here because I've run this a bunch of times.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:43.180
Let me zoom out a little bit so you can see
those.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:48.180
There they are on the genome so I have them
available and I can see where they would bind

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.160
and what sequence they would amplify.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:54.770
Let me go ahead and get rid of those.

00:17:54.770 --> 00:18:03.510
Suppose I want to do some BLAST searching
against this genome.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:07.780
I can go to a different function or widget
and that is the BLAST widget.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:13.980
I could go to tools and run a new BLAST search.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:18.520
I have the ability here to do that and I have
already set this up with parameters that I

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.880
want.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:30.040
I could run the mouse and the cow Adenosine
Deanminase against the genome and I won't

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:35.180
do that but I will go ahead and open this
so you can see what the result looks like.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:40.190
This file is available on the FTP site if
you want that.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:44.660
So here are my results and this is what the
mouse alignment looks like to the genome and

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:47.600
this is what the cow alignment looks like
to the genome.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:53.300
The cow is a little better aligned than the
mouse is.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:19:02.240
That blast RID is available over here and
I can go ahead and add it or if it was a new

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:11.750
one, or one I had not run in this way, I could
show it by adding the RID.

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:16.110
This device is a way of navigating across
the different hits.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:21.460
There is also a separate larger alignment
inspector here.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:25.210
Notice I have two tracks here.

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:29.800
One is the BLAST results themselves and the
other is something called the cleaned alignment

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:34.110
and those are a little bit more useful because
you see how the hits from the same sequence

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:35.480
are connected to each other.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:39.710
I can close that one if I don't want to use
it.

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:46.860
You can use this to navigate across matches
based on the messenger RNA sequence or on

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:50.160
the genomic sequence here.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:54.660
You see the mouse sequence is the one with
a longer accession number and the cow has

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:59.380
a shorter accession number and typically the
cow is a little bit better match but not in

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:02.590
all the exons.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:07.440
Let's go ahead and do one more quick thing
on chromosome 20.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.243
I don't want to do that yet.

00:20:09.243 --> 00:20:13.440
I want to leave chromosome 20 and go to chromosome
2 and we will do a couple of quick things

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:19.370
to show expression and take a look at the
last widget down here on the bottom which

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:22.220
is the assembly region details.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:31.860
I can jump to chromosome two by going over
here and clicking it from the ideogram view

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:36.510
and now I am on chromosome 2 and I can get
rid of the alignment inspector.

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:43.260
I am zoomed way out and I don't want to search
for adenosine deaminase.

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:51.440
I want to look at a region where there is
a mark undernearth the ideogram which tells

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:56.960
you there is alternate assemblies available
for that particular region.

00:20:56.960 --> 00:21:01.120
There also may be some assembly difficulties
in the region and you can look quickly at

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.370
this one which is region 116, near the centromere.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:09.910
It is a region that could be difficult to
assemble.

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:17.220
I am there and I need to add a map that's
going to give me some information about this

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:23.100
particular area so what I want to do is go
to tracks and that track is one of the recommended

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:29.530
tracks which will be Assemblies.

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:34.930
You can see there are number of curation issues
in this region.

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:42.309
There are some clones that don't match the
way we would like.

00:21:42.309 --> 00:21:46.110
These are called discordant clones, these
are clone-end sequences.

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:51.160
Here is the map I wanted to focus on and these
are alternate assemblies for this region that

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:56.600
represent alternative versions of this part
of the genome.

00:21:56.600 --> 00:22:02.050
If you scroll down a little bit on this genome
you see there is a big gap close by.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:09.830
I can take one of these and in assembly region
details we see marks which represent places

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:18.940
where this particular sequence, this alternative
locus for example, this alternative assembly

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:25.230
has relative insertion compared to the genome
and I can switch this around and change it

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:28.800
to the one that is master sort of in this
view.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:37.820
You see when it reloads that the genome has
to be gapped to support that.

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:50.970
Let's do the last thing we came to do today
which is to look at a different place on chromosome

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:57.510
2 which is the region surrounding the lactase
gene.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:04.700
Here is Lactase and I still have the assembly
support tracks up.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:09.770
What I will do here is change that and let's
say we are interested in expression so there

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:13.500
is an expression trackset available here.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:22.210
These are a bunch of Next Generation RNA seq
runs aligned to the genome and are used in

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:27.080
process of annotating things so we can make
splice variants and things like that.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:32.390
And also give you a way of looking at expression
because these are different tissues or organs.

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:35.680
You can count the number that aligned in various
ways.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:41.160
Let me return this to the expression track.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:43.360
Here is sort of the aggregate of this is.

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:48.299
The more interesting thing is to look down
at these which are the individual libraries

00:23:48.299 --> 00:23:56.400
and you see this gene is expressed in duodenum,
and that library is labeled small intestine.

00:23:56.400 --> 00:24:04.070
We would expect Lactase as a digestive enzyme
predominately to be expressed in infants and

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:08.150
in some cases expressed in adult and we will
come back to that idea in a moment.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:14.990
Let me zoom out a little bit and this is a
nice example because you see that Lactase

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:21.240
has specific expression in that particular
organ whereas the genes on either side are

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.990
more ubiquitously expressed.

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:29.179
These are the intron-spanning reads and there
are other ways of thinking about this.

00:24:29.179 --> 00:24:36.610
You can basically look at Exon coverage and
where do these RNA-seq reads align to the

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:42.880
genome and one way to display that is through
the tracks here and I made a set of tracks

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:49.920
by going to the configure menu and loading
them into the browser and basically saving

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:52.320
them into my NCBI account.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:55.540
You can definitely save them if you want to.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.270
We have already done that for you.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:06.370
Here is one that I saved.

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:11.830
Certainly you get similar results and these
are no longer spanning the introns but highlighting

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.570
for you the Exons.

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:18.040
And we also get expression again in the duodenum
library.

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:26.580
The last trick I want to do for you today
is let's change the assembly and load a track

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:30.860
that we have already that is aligned to this.

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:31.860
I will cheat.

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:34.710
There are lots of ways to change the assembly
